status **Approved**, folder `/Interop/Windows_IO/802.1x`. Its **15 authored steps are a person
driving Internet Explorer's certificate-import wizard on a Windows PC**, verbatim:

> Prepare Route and the Supplicant certificate before the test. … They are copied onto
> Supplicant. Start the Internet Explorer on Supplicant. 1) Click Tool button. 2) Click Internet
> option. 3) Click Contents tab. 4) Click Certificate button. ■ Certificate screen 5) Click
> Import button. … 13) Click Finish button. 14) Check that the certificate was added. 15) The
> route certificate is similarly added.  Should be success. See and check the packet sequence.
> When PC and switch are connected, certificate tab is opened. Packet sequence is correct.

and the precondition names the environment outright: `Environment: Windows PC`.

**Why they were not followed.** **There is no Windows PC on tb277, and no GUI of any kind.** The
bench is a Linux testbox driving serial consoles. Steps 1–15 are mouse clicks in a browser that
does not exist here. A **step-level MANUAL-ONLY row already exists** for the certificate-import
limb and still stands after this run.

**What is graded instead, on the Test Engineer's 2026-10-23 decision** to redesign the case with
`wpa_supplicant` on tb277: the authored **objective**, quoted in full because it is the thing
being graded —

> **"Confirm that an authenticator exchanges authentication messages between the supplicant and
> Radius server."**

**Nothing in this log should be read as a claim that the authored steps were executed.**

## What run 2 is, and what it is NOT

Run 1 reached **PARTIAL**. **The DUT behaved correctly at every observed step** — every frame it
sent was a correct RFC 5216 EAP-TLS frame, and it refused a rogue certificate with an EAP-Failure
in 1.045 s. What run 1 could not show was an EAP-TLS authentication **concluding** in EAP-Success.

Run 2 is therefore **not a DUT re-test.** It tests **one hypothesis about our own supplicant**,
written as UNBLOCK option 1 in run 1's verdict:

> The PKCS#12 the DUT exports carries the **CA as well as the leaf**, so wpa_supplicant sends
> both in the client's TLS Certificate message. That makes the client flight 2141 bytes and
> therefore TWO EAP-TLS fragments. `wpa_supplicant` 2.6 predates TLS 1.3 and RFC 9190 while
> OpenSSL 1.1.1 beneath it negotiates TLS 1.3, so it declares the EAP method `Done` with 743
> bytes unsent and DISCARDs the DUT's correct empty ACK. Run 1's control used a single
> certificate, its flight was 1224 bytes in ONE fragment, and that exchange concluded normally.

**So: split the PKCS#12 and present a leaf-only client credential** — `client_cert` and
`private_key` passed separately, the CA kept out of the client chain but still used as
`ca_cert` to validate the SERVER. Target: a one-fragment client flight and **EAP-Success**, with
port1.0.48 reaching Authorized.

No root, no package install, no DUT configuration change beyond the PKI/RADIUS rig run 1 used.

