### Leg R2-A — reproduce run 1's stall in TODAY's window, with the whole PKCS#12

```
tools/tb sudotool tb277 t9032r2 wpasup eth4 --method tls --identity cktls \
    --private-key /tftproot/cktls.p12 --private-key-passwd ckbench2026 \
    --ca-cert /tmp/t9032r2/localca-rsa.cer \
    --expect success --timeout 45 --label R2-A-full-p12 \
    --pcap /tmp/t9032r2/T9032-R2A.pcap --json /tmp/t9032r2/T9032-R2A.json
```

The tail of the exchange, from the tool's own frame table:

```
  0.0417   e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f EAP-Packet   Request   7    TLS    522    0x80 L msglen=2520
  0.0616   00:0d:b9:55:77:0f 01:80:c2:00:00:03 EAP-Packet   Response  7    TLS    1408   0xc0 LM msglen=2141
  0.0626   e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f EAP-Packet   Request   8    TLS    6      0x00 -  <- EMPTY ACK
  30.0292  e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f EAP-Packet   Request   8    TLS    6      0x00 -  <- EMPTY ACK
```

and the supplicant's own reason, from `work/T9032-R2A-wpa_supplicant.log`:

```
SSL: 2141 bytes left to be sent out (of total 2141 bytes)
SSL: sending 1398 bytes, more fragments will follow
EAP-TLS: Done
EAP: Received EAP-Request id=8 method=13 vendor=0 vendorMethod=0
EAP: EAP entering state DISCARD
```

**Identical to run 1 to the byte** (`msglen=2141`, 17 frames, two empty ACKs 30 s apart).
Run 1's finding reproduces exactly five days later. **Leg R2-A is the baseline every other leg
below is compared against.**

### Leg R2-B — the dispatched hypothesis, exactly as written. **It did not work.**

```
tools/tb sudotool tb277 t9032r2 wpasup eth4 --method tls --identity cktls \
    --client-cert /tmp/t9032r2/cktls-leaf.pem --private-key /tmp/t9032r2/cktls-key.pem \
    --ca-cert /tmp/t9032r2/localca-rsa.cer \
    --expect success --timeout 45 --label R2-B-leaf-only \
    --pcap /tmp/t9032r2/T9032-R2B.pcap --json /tmp/t9032r2/T9032-R2B.json
```

The CA was out of the client credential: `client_cert` is a PEM holding **exactly one**
certificate (checked on the box — one `BEGIN CERTIFICATE` line), and the supplicant log confirms
it took the key from a PEM, not from a bundle:

```
OpenSSL: tls_use_private_key_file (PEM) --> loaded
SSL: Private key loaded successfully
```

**And the client flight was still 2141 bytes, to the byte, in two fragments, with the same
stall.** 17 frames, `msglen=2141`, same `EAP-TLS: Done` with 743 bytes unsent, same DISCARD.

🔴 **Why — and this is the finding that run 1 could not have seen.** Removing the CA from
`client_cert` is **not enough**, because OpenSSL puts it back. OpenSSL 1.1.1 builds the client's
certificate chain **automatically from the verify store** when no explicit chain is set, and
`ca_cert` is exactly what loads the DUT's CA into that store. So asking wpa_supplicant to
validate the server with the DUT's CA silently re-attaches that same CA to the *client's* own
chain. **The CA has to leave the verify store too, not just `client_cert`.** Leg R2-C proves
that by removing it.

### Leg R2-C — leaf-only AND an empty verify store: the fragmentation IS fixed

```
tools/tb sudotool tb277 t9032r2 wpasup eth4 --method tls --identity cktls \
    --client-cert /tmp/t9032r2/cktls-leaf.pem --private-key /tmp/t9032r2/cktls-key.pem \
    --expect success --timeout 45 --label R2-C-leaf-only-nostore \
    --pcap /tmp/t9032r2/T9032-R2C.pcap --json /tmp/t9032r2/T9032-R2C.json
```

(no `--ca-cert`; the tool's banner says on every such run that the server certificate is then
accepted **without** validation. That is why this leg is a diagnostic and **not** the leg the
verdict rests on — R2-D below validates the server properly.)

```
SSL: 1262 bytes left to be sent out (of total 1262 bytes)
EAP-TLS: Done
```

The line **`sending 1398 bytes, more fragments will follow` is gone.** On the wire:

| | leg R2-A / R2-B | leg R2-C |
| --- | --- | --- |
| client response id=7 | 1426 B frame, flags **`0xc0 LM`**, `msglen=2141` | 1286 B frame, flags **`0x00`** |
| DUT's next frame (id=8) | 60 B, flags **`0x00`** — an **empty ACK**, asking for the missing fragment | 60 B, flags **`0x80 L`** — **length-included, it carries TLS payload** |

**2141 − 1262 = 879 bytes = the CA's 874 DER bytes + 5 bytes of TLS framing** (3-byte
certificate-list entry length + 2-byte per-entry extensions field that TLS 1.3 adds). The
arithmetic closes exactly, which is the proof that the extra certificate in the flight was the
CA and nothing else.

🔴 **But the exchange still did not conclude**, and the reason is now a *different* one. With
nothing left to fragment, the DUT's id=8 frame is no longer an ACK — it carries data
(flags `0x80 L`). Under TLS 1.3, EAP-TLS (RFC 9190) requires the server to send a protected
**commitment message** after the client's Finished, before EAP-Success. wpa_supplicant 2.6 has
no idea what that is: it has already marked the method `Done` and discards it, twice, 30 s apart:

```
EAP: method process -> ignore=FALSE methodState=DONE decision=UNCOND_SUCC
EAP: Received EAP-Request id=8 method=13 vendor=0 vendorMethod=0
EAP: EAP entering state DISCARD
```

**So the dispatched hypothesis is REFUTED as a cause, and replaced by a sharper one.** The
two-fragment flight was a *symptom of* TLS 1.3, not the cause of the stall. The cause is TLS 1.3
itself, in two separate ways: it makes the client flight bigger (encrypted Certificate +
CertificateVerify + Finished all in the client's half), and it requires a post-Finished
commitment message that a 2016 supplicant cannot answer. Leg R2-D removes the cause.

### 🟢 Leg R2-D — hold the supplicant at TLS 1.2. **EAP-Success.**

wpa_supplicant 2.6 has no `tls_disable_tlsv1_3` network field (it predates it; adding one makes
2.6 reject the whole config file). The TLS ceiling therefore has to come from OpenSSL. It was
set **for this one child process only**, with `OPENSSL_CONF` pointing at a file in the box's
`/tmp` scratch — **no root, no package install, `/etc/ssl/openssl.cnf` untouched, and no other
user of tb277 affected.** `tools/wpasup.py` gained a generic `--env VAR=VALUE` for this.

`/tmp/t9032r2/openssl-tls12.cnf` (kept as `work/openssl-tls12.cnf`):

```
openssl_conf = ck_conf
[ck_conf]
ssl_conf = ck_ssl
[ck_ssl]
system_default = ck_ssl_default
[ck_ssl_default]
MinProtocol = TLSv1.2
MaxProtocol = TLSv1.2
CipherString = DEFAULT@SECLEVEL=1
```

```
tools/tb sudotool tb277 t9032r2 wpasup eth4 --method tls --identity cktls \
    --client-cert /tmp/t9032r2/cktls-leaf.pem --private-key /tmp/t9032r2/cktls-key.pem \
    --ca-cert /tmp/t9032r2/localca-rsa.cer \
    --env OPENSSL_CONF=/tmp/t9032r2/openssl-tls12.cnf \
    --expect success --timeout 45 --label R2-D-tls12-leaf-only \
    --pcap /tmp/t9032r2/T9032-R2D.pcap --json /tmp/t9032r2/T9032-R2D.json
```

The whole exchange, 18 frames, 1.05 s end to end:

```
  t(s)     src               dst               eapol        eap       id   method         eaplen tls
  0.0      00:0d:b9:55:77:0f 01:80:c2:00:00:03 EAPOL-Start  -         -    -              -
  0.0003   e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f EAP-Packet   Request   1    Identity       5
  0.0008   00:0d:b9:55:77:0f 01:80:c2:00:00:03 EAP-Packet   Response  1    Identity       10
  0.0019   e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f EAP-Packet   Request   2    MD5-Challenge  22
  0.0023   00:0d:b9:55:77:0f 01:80:c2:00:00:03 EAP-Packet   Response  2    Legacy-Nak     6
  0.0033   e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f EAP-Packet   Request   3    TLS            6      0x20 S
  0.006    00:0d:b9:55:77:0f 01:80:c2:00:00:03 EAP-Packet   Response  3    TLS            196    0x00 -
  0.0119   e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f EAP-Packet   Request   4    TLS            1014   0xc0 LM msglen=2339
  0.0125   00:0d:b9:55:77:0f 01:80:c2:00:00:03 EAP-Packet   Response  4    TLS            6      0x00 -  <- EMPTY ACK
  0.0134   e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f EAP-Packet   Request   5    TLS            1014   0xc0 LM msglen=2339
  0.0138   00:0d:b9:55:77:0f 01:80:c2:00:00:03 EAP-Packet   Response  5    TLS            6      0x00 -  <- EMPTY ACK
  0.0146   e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f EAP-Packet   Request   6    TLS            341    0x80 L msglen=2339
  0.0391   00:0d:b9:55:77:0f 01:80:c2:00:00:03 EAP-Packet   Response  6    TLS            1408   0xc0 LM msglen=2154
  0.04     e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f EAP-Packet   Request   7    TLS            6      0x00 -  <- EMPTY ACK
  0.0403   00:0d:b9:55:77:0f 01:80:c2:00:00:03 EAP-Packet   Response  7    TLS            762    0x00 -
  0.0427   e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f EAP-Packet   Request   8    TLS            61     0x80 L msglen=51
  0.0439   00:0d:b9:55:77:0f 01:80:c2:00:00:03 EAP-Packet   Response  8    TLS            6      0x00 -  <- EMPTY ACK
  1.0471   e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f EAP-Packet   Success   8    -              4

  EAP-Success frame on the wire: True
RESULT R2-D-tls12-leaf-only: expected success, got success (from wpa_supplicant events) -- MATCH
```

**Read what the DUT did here, because it answers the authored objective directly:**

- it **initiated** the exchange (`EAP-Request/Identity` 0.3 ms after EAPOL-Start);
- it **offered** MD5-Challenge, accepted the supplicant's Legacy-Nak, and **offered TLS**;
- it **fragmented its own 2339-byte flight into three** (`0xc0 LM`, `0xc0 LM`, `0x80 L`) and
  waited for each empty ACK;
- it **reassembled the supplicant's two-fragment 2154-byte flight** — `0xc0 LM msglen=2154`
  then a 762-byte `0x00` remainder — and **ACKed the first fragment correctly**. 🔴 **So a
  two-fragment client flight is handled perfectly by both ends. Fragmentation was never the
  problem**, which is what makes R2-B's negative result worth keeping;
- it ran the RADIUS exchange against its own local server and sent **EAP-Success**.

**The server's certificate chain was validated against the CA exported from this very unit**
(this leg has `--ca-cert`, unlike R2-C):

```
CTRL-EVENT-EAP-PEER-CERT depth=1 subject='/O=Allied Telesis, Inc./CN=AlliedWarePlusCAA2WQS700B'
                                 hash=e9db87ae6a2d8e562e85bcb974d8e513e1721f188057a0af5bd3480b0f34d352
CTRL-EVENT-EAP-PEER-CERT depth=0 subject='/O=Allied-Telesis/CN=AlliedwarePlusSystem/serialNumber=NULL'
                                 hash=e973031b8cc0520d74438d6185d11475fe8773f5bea203c8bd79f86e7260c01d
CTRL-EVENT-EAP-SUCCESS EAP authentication completed successfully
```

